§§ `Chapter 62 -- the remission of ill-will and trespasses`

-- "have completely remitted and pardoned": the text speaks in the past tense of an act the
-- Charter itself does; it is encoded as the entitlement it leaves (fork F62).
-- src:131 | And all the ill-will, hatreds, and bitterness that have arisen between us and our men, clergy and lay, from the date of the quarrel, we have completely remitted and pardoned to everyone.
@ref Chapter 62 clause 62.1 (src:131)
GIVEN `the ill-will, hatreds and bitterness arose between the King and his men, clergy or lay, from the date of the quarrel` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH AN Entitlement
DECIDE `ch 62 -- the ill-will, hatreds and bitterness are remitted and pardoned` IS
    IF `the ill-will, hatreds and bitterness arose between the King and his men, clergy or lay, from the date of the quarrel`
    THEN Entitled
    -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the text does not speak to a case outside the facts this rule names.
    ELSE `Entitlement not covered`

-- src:131 | Moreover, all trespasses occasioned by the said quarrel, from Easter in the sixteenth year of our reign till the restoration of peace, we have fully remitted to all, both clergy and laymen, and completely forgiven, as far as pertains to us.
@ref Chapter 62 clause 62.2 (src:131)
GIVEN `the trespass was occasioned by the said quarrel` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it was committed from Easter in the sixteenth year of the reign till the restoration of peace` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH AN Entitlement
DECIDE `ch 62 -- the trespass is fully remitted and completely forgiven, as far as pertains to the King` IS
    IF   `the trespass was occasioned by the said quarrel`
     AND `it was committed from Easter in the sixteenth year of the reign till the restoration of peace`
    THEN Entitled
    -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the text does not speak to a case outside the facts this rule names.
    ELSE `Entitlement not covered`

§§ `Chapter 63 -- the enjoining clause and the oath`

-- The first limb ("that the English Church be free") restates chapter 1 and is not encoded again
-- (see the jural relations table).  "of us and our heirs": the King's heirs are not a separate party
-- (fork F1).
-- src:133 | Wherefore it is our will, and we firmly enjoin, that the English Church be free, and that the men in our kingdom have and hold all the aforesaid liberties, rights, and concessions, well and peaceably, freely and quietly, fully and wholly, for themselves and their heirs, of us and our heirs, in all respects and in all places for ever, as is aforesaid.
@ref Chapter 63 clause 63.1 (src:133)
GIVEN `the person is a man in the kingdom, or the heir of one` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH AN Entitlement
DECIDE `ch 63 -- the person has and holds all the aforesaid liberties, rights and concessions for himself and his heirs` IS
    IF `the person is a man in the kingdom, or the heir of one`
    THEN Entitled
    -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the text does not speak to a case outside the facts this rule names.
    ELSE `Entitlement not covered`

-- 63.2: an oath is reported to have been taken that the conditions shall be kept; the report is
-- read as stating a duty on the two sides who swore (fork F63).
-- src:133 | An oath, moreover, has been taken, as well on our part as on the part of the barons, that all these conditions aforesaid shall be kept in good faith and without evil intent.
@ref Chapter 63 clause 63.2 (src:133)
GIVEN swearer IS A Party
GIVETH A DEONTIC Party Act
DECIDE `ch 63 -- all these conditions shall be kept in good faith and without evil intent` IS
    IF   swearer EQUALS `the King`
      OR swearer EQUALS `the barons`
    THEN PARTY swearer
         MUST `keep all the conditions of the Charter in good faith and without evil intent`
         HENCE FULFILLED
         LEST BREACH BY swearer BECAUSE "ch 63: a party to the oath did not keep the conditions of the Charter in good faith and without evil intent"
    ELSE FULFILLED
